<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joscelin II of Edessa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1159</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joscelin II was the last count of Edessa, succeeding his father Joscelin I in 1131. While he was absent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a> took the city of Edessa on 24 December 1144, which prompted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>. Joscelin failed to retake it in 1146, was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>'s forces in 1150 and died in prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joscelin-ii-of-edessa.jpg" alt="Joscelin II of Edessa" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous 13th-century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Histoire_dOutremer-Boulogne-Ms142-f153v_(cropped_Raymond_of_Poitiers_Count_of_Antioch_and_Josselin_II_Count_of_Edessa_playing_chess).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
